# wfg-log 1
# fixture first-go — a cue fired, played and finished, hand-written and hand-checked.
#
# The guarantee this exists for is the one the whole Player interface was shaped
# around: a performance recorded on a machine with an audio interface has to
# reproduce, record for record, on one without. Every line below is either the
# operator asking for something or the machine reporting what happened, and
# `wfg replay --bundle` re-applies all of it with no Tracktion, no device and no
# sound.
#
# It needs the bundle because GO reads standby out of the show. It does NOT need
# the media: with no audio side there is nothing to arm, so the file is never
# looked for — which is worth knowing, because it means this fixture stays valid
# whether or not anybody commits a WAV beside it.
#
# The graph came up. Four voices, two outputs, thirty-six nodes.
A 0 0 engine audio.editBuilt s:"hosted" i:4 i:2 i:36
#
# The operator armed the standby cue ahead of time, which is what a show does
# while they read the next line. The run identifier is the argument the caller
# left out and the engine drew, written back into the record so a replay
# re-supplies it rather than drawing a different one.
A 40 1 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 audio.arm s:"B3N8R5TW" s:"G7W9EF36"
#
# The audio side found it a voice.
A 42 2 engine audio.armed s:"G7W9EF36" i:0
#
# GO. Standby advances to the second cue whatever happens next (§3.5), and the
# run that was already armed is the one that launches — armed is not running.
A 100 3 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 go s:"G7W9EF36"
#
# The sound started, two ticks later, which is the launch latency at this block
# size and rate. Reported on the tick it was observed.
A 102 4 engine run.started s:"G7W9EF36"
#
# A second GO, on the cue standby moved to. A different cue, so a different run
# with an identifier of its own.
A 140 5 udp:10.0.0.5:9000 go s:"M5TQ7XVA"
#
# It failed, and said which failure it was: the bundle has no rain.wav. An
# APPLIED command that could not be honoured is not a rejected one - the request
# was legal and the show was not in a state to do it - and a show with one
# missing sound is still a show somebody has to run tonight.
A 141 6 engine run.failed s:"M5TQ7XVA" s:"media-missing"
#
# A report about a run nobody created is refused rather than inventing one.
R 150 7 cli unknown-id run.started s:"NOSUCHID"
#
# The first cue finished on its own, at the end of its file. Its voice is free
# from this tick.
A 260 8 engine run.ended s:"G7W9EF36"
#
# And killing something already finished is applied and does nothing. An
# operator hitting the button twice has not made a mistake, and a client that
# reconnected has no way to know what is still running.
A 300 9 cli run.kill s:"G7W9EF36"
